WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:11.100
AWS Public Change Alerting matches public AWS announcements to
configured services and risk phrases, then sends route-scoped
candidates to Slack.

2
00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:18.320
Each candidate preserves the matched text, potentially
relevant environments, and exact configuration release.

3
00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:25.540
The architecture keeps feed checkpoints, queued work, and
Slack outcomes traceable through delivery and recovery.

4
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.950
EventBridge schedules three Lambdas.

5
00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:37.645
The watcher fetches approved feeds, matches service aliases
and risk phrases, and records candidates before advancing
checkpoints.

6
00:00:37.765 --> 00:00:42.015
The dispatcher sends due delivery records to SQS FIFO.

7
00:00:42.135 --> 00:00:47.235
The queue invokes the Slack worker, which sends the message
and records the outcome.

8
00:00:47.355 --> 00:00:53.405
The reconciler recovers overdue work and marks expired sends
as delivery unknown.

9
00:00:53.525 --> 00:00:59.500
A fifth Lambda, shadow, evaluates the same feed policy in
memory for deployment checks.

10
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:06.075
DynamoDB keeps checkpoints and delivery history after queue
messages are consumed.

11
00:01:06.195 --> 00:01:11.970
Conditional writes coordinate leases, deduplication, and
retries across Lambdas.

12
00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:15.940
SQS provides transport and destination ordering.

13
00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:22.085
S3 holds exact configuration releases and retained feed
responses for replay.

14
00:01:22.205 --> 00:01:28.655
CloudWatch collects runtime logs and metrics; alarms notify
the operator through SNS.

15
00:01:28.775 --> 00:01:35.225
CloudTrail has a narrower role here: proving which table and
timestamp a recovery request used.

16
00:01:35.345 --> 00:01:37.820
It doesn't prove restored contents.

17
00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:44.740
Terraform defines the resources and scoped IAM roles,
including access to Slack credentials.

18
00:01:45.733 --> 00:01:54.308
M1's live cohort produced twelve Slack posts, each with one
network attempt, and twelve posted records in DynamoDB.

19
00:01:54.428 --> 00:02:03.428
A separate recovery exercise completed a pending delivery and
moved an expired send lease to delivery unknown without
another Slack call.

20
00:02:03.548 --> 00:02:07.423
The fixed load run created fifty records in ten minutes.

21
00:02:07.543 --> 00:02:10.493
The owner also received the alarm email.

22
00:02:11.466 --> 00:02:15.291
M2 addressed what happens as history accumulates.

23
00:02:15.411 --> 00:02:22.936
The migration added expiry dates to one-hundred-and-sixty-four
old rows, leaving the four active checkpoints alone.

24
00:02:23.056 --> 00:02:29.806
Retained-source replay now binds one saved response and
inspected state into a reviewed plan.

25
00:02:29.926 --> 00:02:33.801
It can fill missing records but can't touch feed checkpoints.

26
00:02:33.921 --> 00:02:36.521
No live source replay was applied.

27
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:46.750
The matcher scored twenty-nine true-positive service and risk
pairs, with no false positives or false negatives, on forty-
seven labeled items.

28
00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:49.395
That passes the global thresholds.

29
00:02:49.515 --> 00:02:58.715
But six configured pairs have no historical positive, so that
score doesn't establish recall on real AWS wording for those
pairs.

30
00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:04.600
M3 reused earlier evidence where the changes still supported
it.

31
00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.720
The missing test was application rollback.

32
00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:15.415
With durable execution paused, we switched all five Lambdas to
the qualified predecessor and back to the successor.

33
00:03:15.535 --> 00:03:22.635
One shadow run per version returned the same eight candidate
identities from two-hundred-and-forty items.

34
00:03:23.633 --> 00:03:28.108
The stopped-state digests matched, then we restored normal
operation.

35
00:03:28.228 --> 00:03:35.053
The fixed observation window passed, all twenty-eight alarms
were healthy, and Terraform showed no changes.

36
00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:42.748
The owner accepted readiness for one environment and Slack
destination at three-hundred delivery requests per hour.

37
00:03:42.868 --> 00:03:46.543
Live service on restored tables remains unproved.

38
00:03:47.533 --> 00:03:51.208
M4 qualified supervised short dev windows.

39
00:03:51.328 --> 00:03:58.978
Step Functions owns the deadline; CodeBuild applies reviewed
Terraform plans to activate and park the service.

40
00:03:59.098 --> 00:04:10.923
The fixed twenty-minute observation recorded one watcher,
twenty dispatcher, and four reconciler invocations, with
matching heartbeats and no scheduled-function errors or
throttles.

41
00:04:11.043 --> 00:04:15.268
It produced no new Slack posts and ended without extension.

42
00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:24.888
Deadline cleanup survived local waiter loss, disabling four
triggers, fencing five functions, and removing twenty-eight
alarms and the dashboard.

43
00:04:25.008 --> 00:04:32.958
An operator remains available through parked verification;
unattended notification qualification is still pending.

44
00:04:33.833 --> 00:04:39.058
M5 tightened four maintenance checks without activating AWS.

45
00:04:39.178 --> 00:04:49.553
Tracked agent adapters reject credential-bearing
configuration, and CLI help tests tolerate terminal wrapping
while still requiring the documented wording.

46
00:04:49.673 --> 00:04:55.698
A stable synthetic package manifest is checked separately from
current-source verification.

47
00:04:55.818 --> 00:05:03.093
Feed-claim tests now cover expired-lease URL mismatch and
exact DynamoDB update and read counts.

48
00:05:03.213 --> 00:05:08.663
These changes preserve the service contracts and supervised
operating boundary.
